        "severity": "medium",
        "category": "bug",
        "title": "Removing touch-action: none breaks pointer dragging on touch devices",
        "explanation": "The draggable element no longer sets touch-action: none. On touch devices the browser now treats a finger drag as a pan or scroll, so it fires pointercancel and stops sending pointermove events once the gesture starts. useDrag has no pointercancel handler, so the drag stops mid-gesture and the press state is left dangling. The user-select: none that was always set is also gone; it is now applied only after the 5px threshold.",
        "suggestion": "Keep touch-action: none on the draggable element, either inline or in a CSS rule for this element, and consider handling pointercancel by calling cancel().",
        "evidence": "<div {...props} {...drag} ref={ref}>",
